::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2x + 5 = 17. {
~A. x = 11 #This subtracts only one number and does not follow both inverse steps.
=B. x = 6 #Correct. Subtract 5 to get 2x = 12, then divide by 2.
~C. x = 12 #This stops after subtracting 5 and does not divide by 2.
~D. x = 34 #This multiplies instead of isolating x.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 3a - 4 = 20. {
=A. a = 8 #Correct. Add 4 to get 3a = 24, then divide by 3.
~B. a = 6 #This divides too early and ignores the -4.
~C. a = 24 #This stops after adding 4 and does not divide by 3.
~D. a = 16 #This subtracts 4 instead of adding 4.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 5n + 6 = 31. {
~A. n = 25 #This stops after subtracting 6 and does not divide by 5.
~B. n = 7 #This adds 6 before dividing.
=C. n = 5 #Correct. Subtract 6 to get 5n = 25, then divide by 5.
~D. n = 37 #This adds 6 instead of subtracting 6.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 4p - 9 = 15. {
~A. p = 1 #This divides too early and ignores the -9.
~B. p = 24 #This stops after adding 9 and does not divide by 4.
~C. p = 15 #This repeats the right side without solving.
=D. p = 6 #Correct. Add 9 to get 4p = 24, then divide by 4.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 6x + 2 = 38. {
~A. x = 36 #This stops after subtracting 2 and does not divide by 6.
~B. x = 40 #This adds 2 instead of subtracting 2.
~C. x = 228 #This multiplies instead of isolating x.
=D. x = 6 #Correct. Subtract 2 to get 6x = 36, then divide by 6.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 7y - 5 = 30. {
~A. y = 25 #This subtracts 5 and stops before dividing.
=B. y = 5 #Correct. Add 5 to get 7y = 35, then divide by 7.
~C. y = 35 #This stops after adding 5 and does not divide by 7.
~D. y = 210 #This multiplies instead of using inverse operations.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -3m + 4 = 19. {
=A. m = -5 #Correct. Subtract 4 to get -3m = 15, then divide by -3.
~B. m = 5 #This uses the wrong sign after dividing by -3.
~C. m = 23 #This adds 4 instead of subtracting it.
~D. m = 15 #This stops after subtracting 4 and does not divide by -3.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -2q - 8 = 10. {
~A. q = -1 #This divides too early and ignores the -8.
~B. q = -18 #This stops after adding 8 and does not divide by -2.
=C. q = -9 #Correct. Add 8 to get -2q = 18, then divide by -2.
~D. q = 9 #This has the wrong sign after dividing by -2.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve <sup>x</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> + 5 = 11. {
~A. x = 6 #This stops after subtracting 5 and does not multiply by 3.
=B. x = 18 #Correct. Subtract 5, then multiply by 3.
~C. x = 33 #This multiplies before removing the +5.
~D. x = 48 #This adds 5 and then multiplies by 3.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve <sup>a</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> - 2 = 7. {
~A. a = 20 #This subtracts 2 instead of adding 2.
~B. a = 9 #This stops after adding 2 and does not multiply by 4.
=C. a = 36 #Correct. Add 2, then multiply by 4.
~D. a = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #This divides by 4 instead of multiplying by 4.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve <sup>r</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> + 1 = 9. {
~A. r = 8 #This stops after subtracting 1 and does not multiply by 5.
~B. r = 50 #This adds 1 before multiplying.
~C. r = <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> #This divides by 5 instead of multiplying by 5.
=D. r = 40 #Correct. Subtract 1, then multiply by 5.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve <sup>t</sup>&frasl;<sub>-4</sub> + 3 = 8. {
~A. t = 20 #This uses the wrong sign when multiplying by -4.
=B. t = -20 #Correct. Subtract 3, then multiply by -4.
~C. t = 5 #This stops after subtracting 3 and does not multiply by -4.
~D. t = -11 #This does not undo the division by -4.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step to solve 4x + 7 = 31? {
=A. Subtract 7 from both sides #Correct. Remove the added constant before dividing by 4.
~B. Divide both sides by 4 #This divides too early before removing +7.
~C. Add 7 to both sides #This repeats the addition instead of undoing it.
~D. Multiply both sides by 4 #This does not isolate x.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step to solve 6n - 8 = 28? {
~A. Divide both sides by 6 #This divides too early before removing -8.
~B. Subtract 8 from both sides #This repeats the subtraction instead of undoing it.
~C. Multiply both sides by 6 #This does not isolate n.
=D. Add 8 to both sides #Correct. Addition undoes the subtraction of 8.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>After subtracting 9 from both sides of 5x + 9 = 34, what equation remains? {
~A. x = 25 #This drops the coefficient 5.
~B. 5x = 43 #This adds 9 instead of subtracting 9.
=C. 5x = 25 #Correct. 34 - 9 = 25.
~D. 5x = 34 #This does not remove the +9.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>After adding 6 to both sides of 3y - 6 = 18, what equation remains? {
~A. y = 24 #This drops the coefficient 3.
=B. 3y = 24 #Correct. 18 + 6 = 24.
~C. 3y = 12 #This subtracts 6 instead of adding 6.
~D. 3y = 18 #This does not remove the -6.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Which value checks correctly for 2x + 3 = 15? {
=A. x = 6 #Correct. 2(6) + 3 = 15.
~B. x = 9 #2(9) + 3 is 21, not 15.
~C. x = 12 #2(12) + 3 is 27, not 15.
~D. x = 15 #2(15) + 3 is 33, not 15.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Which value checks correctly for 4a - 1 = 19? {
~A. a = 4 #4(4) - 1 is 15, not 19.
~B. a = 18 #4(18) - 1 is not 19.
=C. a = 5 #Correct. 4(5) - 1 = 19.
~D. a = 20 #4(20) - 1 is not 19.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Which value checks correctly for -3n + 2 = -10? {
~A. n = -4 #-3(-4) + 2 is 14, not -10.
~B. n = -2 #-3(-2) + 2 is 8, not -10.
~C. n = 10 #-3(10) + 2 is -28, not -10.
=D. n = 4 #Correct. -3(4) + 2 = -10.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Which value checks correctly for <sup>p</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + 6 = 13? {
=A. p = 14 #Correct. 14 divided by 2 plus 6 equals 13.
~B. p = 7 #7 divided by 2 plus 6 is not 13.
~C. p = 13 #13 divided by 2 plus 6 is not 13.
~D. p = 19 #19 divided by 2 plus 6 is not 13.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves 3x + 6 = 24 and stops at 3x = 18. What is the missing step? {
~A. Add 6 to both sides #The +6 has already been removed.
=B. Divide both sides by 3 #Correct. The coefficient 3 still needs to be undone.
~C. Subtract 3 from both sides #Subtraction does not undo multiplication by 3.
~D. Stop because x = 18 #3x = 18 does not isolate x.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves 2y - 5 = 13 by dividing 13 by 2 first. What is the error? {
~A. The student should multiply by 2 first. #Multiplying first does not remove -5.
~B. The student should subtract 5 again. #That repeats subtraction instead of undoing it.
=C. The student should add 5 before dividing by 2. #Correct. Remove the constant term before undoing the coefficient.
~D. There is no error. #Dividing first ignores the -5.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves 4m + 3 = 27 and gets m = 24. What is the error? {
=A. The student stopped after subtracting 3 and did not divide by 4. #Correct. 4m = 24, so m = 6.
~B. The student added 3 instead of subtracting 3. #m = 24 comes from subtracting 3 and stopping.
~C. The student divided by 4 too early. #The student did not divide by 4 at all.
~D. There is no error. #4(24) + 3 is not 27.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves <sup>x</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> + 2 = 8 and gets x = 6. What is the error? {
~A. The student added 2 instead of subtracting 2. #The value 6 comes from subtracting 2.
~B. The student divided by 5 too early. #The student did not undo division by 5.
~C. There is no error. #6 divided by 5 plus 2 is not 8.
=D. The student stopped after subtracting 2 and did not multiply by 5. #Correct. x divided by 5 equals 6, so x = 30.
}

::ALG1_U02_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Why do two-step equations usually undo addition or subtraction before multiplication or division? {
=A. Because it reverses the order used to build the expression #Correct. Remove the constant first, then undo the coefficient or divisor.
~B. Because multiplication and division are never used #Multiplication and division are used after the constant is removed.
~C. Because the variable should stay with the constant #The goal is to isolate the variable.
~D. Because checking replaces solving #Checking verifies the solution after solving.
}
